@node CMacs AI and ai-glib 0.13
@chapter CMacs AI and ai-glib 0.13: ACP, Steering, Limits

@cindex ACP
@cindex Agent Client Protocol
@cindex steering a running turn
@cindex session limit
@cindex rate limit
@cindex codex app-server

ai-glib went from 0.10.2 to 0.13.0 underneath cmacs, and four of the
things it gained change what you can do from the editor rather than
only what the library can do.  This chapter is all four in one place,
because they landed together and lean on each other.

@itemize @bullet
@item
@strong{ACP agents are providers.}  Any program speaking the Agent
Client Protocol becomes a provider symbol, named by its command line in
@code{cmacs-ai-acp-agents}.
@item
@strong{Codex can run as an app-server.}
@code{cmacs-ai-codex-transport} opts into @command{codex app-server},
which is what makes a codex turn steerable.
@item
@strong{Limits are not failures.}  A usage limit, a rate limit, a
lapsed login and an empty balance are told apart, said in a sentence
that names the reset time, and a brigade run stopped by one is parked
rather than failed.
@item
@strong{A running turn takes more words.}  Typing into a harness that
is still working hands the words to that turn; a message sent to a
running in-process brigade agent goes into its tool loop.  Both fall
back to ``wait for the next turn'' only when the engine refuses.
@end itemize

Two things in ai-glib 0.13 were @emph{not} ported, on purpose; see
@ref{AI 0.13 Deferred}.

@menu
* AI 0.13 ACP Agents::          Any ACP agent as a provider.
* AI 0.13 Codex Transport::     @code{exec} or @code{app-server}.
* AI 0.13 Limits::              Usage limits are not failures.
* AI 0.13 Steering::            Words into a running turn.
* AI 0.13 Deferred::            What was deliberately not ported.
* AI 0.13 Reference::           Options, functions, tests.
@end menu

@node AI 0.13 ACP Agents
@section ACP agents

@subsection What ACP is here

The Agent Client Protocol (@url{https://agentclientprotocol.com}) is one
wire protocol many coding agents speak over stdio.  ai-glib's
@code{AiAcpClient} starts the agent's command for each turn, speaks ACP
v1 (JSON-RPC 2.0, one object per line), and translates what the agent
says into the same response and event stream every other provider
produces.  cmacs exposes it as the provider @code{acp}.

ACP has no request that selects a model.  The agent's own
configuration, or its command line, chooses one; ai-glib reports the
single model id @code{default}.  So the thing that tells two ACP agents
apart is not a model string, it is the command that starts them ---
which is exactly the shape of the problem
@code{cmacs-ai-openai-compatible-endpoints} already solved for HTTP
servers, and it is solved the same way.

@subsection Naming your agents

@lisp
(setq cmacs-ai-acp-agents
      '((gemini-acp :command "gemini --experimental-acp")
        (my-agent   :command "my-agent acp --stdio"
                    :skip-permissions t)))
@end lisp

@vindex cmacs-ai-acp-agents
Each entry is @code{(@var{name} . @var{plist})}, and each @var{name} is
a provider symbol everywhere a built-in name is: it completes in
@code{cmacs-ai-chat-with-provider}, can be
@code{cmacs-ai-harness-provider} or @code{cmacs-ai-textops-provider},
and can be a brigade agent's model prefix (@samp{gemini-acp/default}).

@table @code
@item :command
The agent's command line as one string.  Split the way a shell would,
with no shell run: quoting works, @samp{$VAR}, pipes and redirections
do not.
@item :skip-permissions
Non-@code{nil} answers every permission request ``allow once'' and lets
the agent write files through the client.  Absent or @code{nil}, every
request is refused.
@item :client-fs
@code{nil} stops cmacs serving the agent's file requests at all.
Absent means on.
@end table

An entry without @code{:command} runs whatever the
@env{AI_ACP_COMMAND} environment variable names, which is also what the
bare @code{acp} provider does.  A flag an entry does not mention keeps
ai-glib's default, and the defaults are the cautious ones: permissions
refused, because there is nobody at a prompt to ask; file reads served,
confined to the working directory.

A name that appears both here and in
@code{cmacs-ai-openai-compatible-endpoints} is the endpoint.  That is
the order the lookup was in before ACP existed, so adding an agent can
never change what an existing name means.

@subsection What an ACP agent cannot do

@itemize @bullet
@item
@strong{Sign in.}  ai-glib never runs ACP's @code{authenticate}.  The
agent must already be logged in.
@item
@strong{Be steered.}  The protocol accepts no input mid-turn but a
cancel.  Words typed while an ACP turn runs wait for the next one.
@item
@strong{Use cmacs's tool array.}  Like every CLI client it runs its own
tools in its own process.  In a harness it is handed an MCP config
naming this cmacs, the way every CLI agent is, and ai-glib passes those
servers to the agent when the session opens --- so whether it reaches
cmacs's tools is up to the agent's own MCP support, and this has not
been tried against a real one.  Under the brigade's in-process worker
there is no such config: an ACP agent there has the agent's tools and
none of the brigade's.
@item
@strong{Run a terminal for the agent.}  ai-glib never offers ACP's
@code{terminal} capability: it would be a second, unapproved way to
execute what the agent's own tools already run behind a permission
request.
@end itemize

@subsection ACP in the brigade

@code{cmacs-brigade-cli-providers} maps a CLI provider to the
subprocess worker that launches it.  @code{acp} is deliberately absent
from it.  An ACP agent is spoken to over JSON-RPC by ai-glib, not run
as @samp{PROGRAM < prompt}, so there is no subprocess form for a worker
to launch; it runs under the @code{inproc} worker through ai-glib's
client.

@subsection Where it is wired

For anyone adding the next provider: cmacs keeps @emph{two} symbol
tables, and a provider present in one and missing from the other works
in a chat buffer and is ``unknown provider'' in @code{cmacs-ai-call}.

@multitable @columnfractions .40 .25 .35
@headitem Table @tab File @tab Reached by
@item @code{cmacs_ai_provider_type_from_symbol}
@tab @file{cmacs/ai/cmacs-ai-config.c}
@tab @code{cmacs-ai-call}, @code{cmacs-ai-list-models}, the harness
@item @code{cmacs_ai__make_client}
@tab @file{cmacs/ai/cmacs-ai-client.c}
@tab @code{cmacs-ai-client-new}, chat buffers, the in-process worker
@end multitable

Settings a type alone does not carry --- an endpoint's URL, an agent's
command line, the codex transport --- are applied by one function,
@code{cmacs_ai_apply_provider_settings}, called from all three
construction sites.  @code{cmacs-ai-client-cli-option} reads a client's
properties back (@code{"command"}, @code{"skip-permissions"},
@code{"client-fs"}, @code{"transport"}), which is how the tests prove a
setting arrived rather than assuming it.

@node AI 0.13 Codex Transport
@section The codex transport

@vindex cmacs-ai-codex-transport
@lisp
(setq cmacs-ai-codex-transport 'app-server)   ; default: 'exec
@end lisp

@multitable @columnfractions .25 .45 .30
@headitem Value @tab Runs @tab Steerable
@item @code{exec} @tab @command{codex exec --json} @tab no
@item @code{app-server} @tab @command{codex app-server} @tab yes
@end multitable

@code{exec} is the default and is what every earlier cmacs did.
@code{app-server} is JSON-RPC over stdio, one process per turn, and a
steer is a @code{turn/steer} request naming the running turn.

It is opt-in for a reason you should know before choosing it: ai-glib
built that transport from Codex's published protocol source and has run
it only against a scripted stand-in.  @strong{It has never been run
against a real codex.}  Treat it as unverified until it has worked for
you.

The setting applies wherever cmacs builds a codex client through
ai-glib: a chat, @code{cmacs-ai-call}, the harness, an in-process
brigade agent, and the configured default provider when that is codex.
It does not affect the brigade's @code{codex} subprocess worker, which
runs @command{codex exec} itself.

@node AI 0.13 Limits
@section Limits are not failures

@subsection Four conditions that used to be one string

A CLI reports a usage limit, a 429 and an expired login as the same
thing: a failed run.  ai-glib 0.13 reads what the CLI said and gives
each a real error code, and for the two that are a wait it works out
@emph{when} the wait ends.

@multitable @columnfractions .18 .32 .35 .15
@headitem Kind @tab ai-glib code @tab What it means @tab Waiting fixes it
@item @code{session-limit} @tab @code{AI_ERROR_SESSION_LIMIT}
@tab The account's usage allowance is spent until it resets. @tab yes
@item @code{rate-limited} @tab @code{AI_ERROR_RATE_LIMITED}
@tab Too many requests; slow down. @tab yes
@item @code{signed-out} @tab @code{AI_ERROR_INVALID_API_KEY}
@tab Not logged in, or the credentials were refused. @tab no
@item @code{quota} @tab @code{AI_ERROR_INSUFFICIENT_QUOTA}
@tab Out of credit, or the plan does not cover this. @tab no
@end multitable

A session limit is not a failed request at all: the CLI never contacts
the API.  It writes one message of its own and exits.  Retrying before
the stated reset cannot succeed, which is why it has to be
distinguishable before anything decides whether to try again.

@subsection The notice line

So that a supervisor watching a log can see a pause without parsing a
localised sentence with no year in it, ai-glib puts a machine-readable
token inside the error text:

@example
the account's session usage limit is reached; it resets at 18:50 on
Friday 20 November ai-glib-session-limit: reset=1795218600
@end example

@noindent
(one line; wrapped here).  That token is for programs.  cmacs reads the
reset time out of it and then removes it from everything a person sees.

@subsection One classifier

@findex cmacs-ai-error-classify
@code{cmacs_ai_error_classify} in @file{cmacs/ai/cmacs-ai-stream.c}
maps a @code{GError} to a kind, a reset time and the cleaned message.
It is pure --- no Lisp, callable from any thread --- and exposed as
@code{cmacs-ai-error-classify} so it can be tested and so Lisp holding
only an error's text can ask the same question the same way:

@lisp
(cmacs-ai-error-classify
 'rate-limited
 "codex is rate limited ai-glib-session-limit: reset=1900000000 \
(codex: slow down)")
;; @result{} (rate-limited 1900000000
;;     "codex is rate limited (codex: slow down)")
@end lisp

A notice found under any @emph{other} code, or outside ai-glib's error
domain, still classifies as @code{session-limit}.  The tool loop and
the conversation layer both re-wrap a provider's error on the way up,
and a wrapper that lost the code must not turn ``paused until 18:50''
into ``failed''.

@subsection The payload, and why it is safe

Every asynchronous error payload was @code{(:error @var{message})}.  A
classified one is now

@example
(:error @var{message} :kind @var{kind} :reset @var{timestamp-or-nil})
@end example

@var{message} is still second, so a caller reading @code{(cadr
payload)} or @code{(plist-get payload :error)} is unaffected, and an
error that does not classify keeps exactly the two-element shape it
always had.  This applies to streamed and non-streamed chat replies,
the tool loop (@code{cmacs-ai-tools-run-async}), a single off-thread
tool call, and the harness's @code{:error} event.  The synchronous
paths that signal @code{cmacs-ai-error} carry the cleaned message.

@subsection What you see

@findex cmacs-ai-error-sentence
@code{cmacs-ai-error-sentence} is the one place the classification
becomes words, so a chat, the harness and the brigade describe one
condition one way:

@example
Session limit reached; resets 14:30.  (claude: Max plan)
Session limit reached; resets 09:00 on Sat 21 Nov.
Rate limited; try again after 14:31.
Not signed in, or the credentials were refused; sign in to the
provider again.
@end example

A reset later today is a clock time; any other day adds the weekday and
date, because ``resets 09:00'' read at 23:00 means tomorrow and says
today.  The provider's own words follow in parentheses: the plan name
or the login command is usually in those, and it is the part you act
on.

In a chat the sentence is the body of the @samp{error} heading.  In a
harness it is written into the transcript as well as the echo area ---
``when can I use this again'' is a thing to be able to scroll back to
--- and the raw notice inside ai-glib's own error block is hidden with
a @code{display} property rather than deleted, because the block's
style spans are byte offsets into the text as rendered.

@subsection In the brigade

An in-process run stopped by a session or rate limit is @emph{parked},
not failed:

@itemize @bullet
@item
the task goes to @code{waiting-input} with the sentence above as its
reason;
@item
its conversation, session and --- for an isolated agent --- its
worktree and everything it edited are kept;
@item
@code{cmacs-brigade-run-finished-functions} does @emph{not} fire,
because the run has not ended; the per-turn hook does;
@item
it is not restarted before the reset.  Mail sent to it while it is
parked waits, and a timer delivers it when the limit lifts.
@end itemize

That last point is not a nicety.  Without it a parked task with a
message waiting would be started, refused at once, parked, and kicked
again --- a loop at the speed of a failing request.

@vindex cmacs-brigade-limit-retry-seconds
When the provider did not say when the limit lifts,
@code{cmacs-brigade-limit-retry-seconds} (900) is the wait.  It is
deliberately not short: every retry into a limit is a refused request.

The turn that was refused is not re-sent by itself.  Its prompt is
already in the session, so any message --- ``carry on'' --- continues
from it.  Sending one automatically would be the brigade deciding to
spend the first of a fresh allowance on its own.

A lapsed login and an empty balance still end the run @code{failed}:
waiting fixes neither.

@findex cmacs-ai-session-limit-scan
@strong{Subprocess workers} (@code{claude-code}, @code{opencode},
@code{codex}) are CLIs the brigade spawned itself, so they never pass
through ai-glib's classification.  On a failed exit their output is
scanned with the same parser (@code{cmacs-ai-session-limit-scan}):
ai-glib's notice line is taken at its word, and a CLI's own sentence
counts only when it both names a limit and states a reset ai-glib can
read.  A match makes the failure reason say ``session limit, resets
14:30'' instead of ``worker exited 1''.  It still ends @code{failed},
unlike the in-process worker: a subprocess turn that never ran left no
session to resume, and a follow-up message would start a CLI holding
that message and none of the agent's instructions.  Retrying the task
rebuilds the prompt properly.

@subsection Redirects are refused

@cindex Redirect refused
ai-glib 0.13 no longer follows an HTTP redirect that would carry your
API key or request body to another address.  An
@code{openai-compatible} endpoint that only worked because a redirect
was silently followed now fails:

@example
Redirect refused (HTTP 301): a request that carries ...
@end example

The remedy is the URL, and the chat says so under the error: set the
provider's base URL to where the redirect points.  For an entry in
@code{cmacs-ai-openai-compatible-endpoints} that is its
@code{:base-url}.  The usual causes are @samp{http://} where the server
wants @samp{https://}, and a missing @samp{/v1}.

@node AI 0.13 Steering
@section Steering: words into a running turn

@subsection The problem

Without steering, a message for a turn that is running waits and
becomes the next turn.  A correction --- ``stop, use the staging
database'' --- arrives after the work it was meant to change has
finished, and a follow-up costs a whole extra turn that rereads
everything.

ai-glib's @code{AiSteerable} hands the words to the turn that is
running, at the next point the engine itself accepts input: before its
next model call, or at the top of the next round of a tool loop.
Nothing is cancelled, interrupted or restarted to make room.

@subsection The answer is a tri-state, and the third value is the one that matters

@multitable @columnfractions .20 .55 .25
@headitem Result @tab Means @tab Queue the words again?
@item @code{delivered} @tab The running turn took the words. @tab no
@item @code{indeterminate} @tab Sent, and nothing confirmed it.
@tab @strong{no}
@item @code{refused} @tab Provably not delivered.  A reason says why.
@tab yes
@end multitable

@code{indeterminate} counts as delivered.  The words may already be
running, and running them twice is the one mistake steering must never
make.  Every caller in cmacs follows the same rule: re-queue on
@code{refused} and on nothing else.

@subsection Which engines can be steered

@multitable @columnfractions .38 .17 .45
@headitem Engine @tab Steerable @tab How
@item @code{claude-code} @tab yes
@tab a stream-json user event on stdin, kept open for the turn
@item @code{claude-tmux} @tab yes
@tab a line typed into the TUI while it works
@item any HTTP provider with local tools @tab yes
@tab spliced into cmacs's own tool loop at the top of the next round
@item @code{codex-cli} @tab on @code{app-server} only
@tab @code{turn/steer}
@item @code{opencode}, @code{grok-build}, @code{cursor},
@code{antigravity}, ACP @tab no
@tab no seam: the protocol takes no input mid-turn
@end multitable

The HTTP row is how providers whose clients cannot be steered get
steering at all: the tool loop running them can, so the loop is what is
steered.  The words go in as one user message after every result of
the batch of tools that just ran --- never between a tool call and its
result.

@subsection In a harness buffer

@kbd{C-c C-c} while a turn is in flight used to say ``still working''.
Now it steers:

@enumerate
@item
The line leaves the prompt and is handed to the running turn.
@item
On @code{delivered} a small note appears in the transcript:
@samp{steered: use the staging db}.
@item
On @code{indeterminate} the note says @samp{(unconfirmed)} and the echo
area explains; nothing is queued.
@item
On @code{refused}, or when the engine cannot be steered at all, the
line is queued, the mode line shows @samp{[1 queued]}, and it goes as
an ordinary turn the moment the session is idle.
@end enumerate

Three details that are easy to get wrong and are tested:

@itemize @bullet
@item
@strong{A slash command is not steered.}  Steering sends text as
written --- nothing resolves a command --- so @samp{/clear} would reach
the model as a word.  It stays in the prompt.
@item
@strong{Stopping a turn does not start the queued one.}  @kbd{C-c C-k}
empties the queue first and puts the lines back in the prompt.
@item
@strong{Answers are matched by ticket}, not by order: two steers in
flight can come back out of order.
@end itemize

@vindex cmacs-ai-harness-steerable
@code{cmacs-ai-harness-steerable} (default @code{t}) is what makes a
Claude Code session steerable.  It changes how the CLI is started:
stdin is left open for the length of the turn.  A real @command{claude}
is unaffected; a wrapper script standing in for it that reads its whole
stdin before answering would wait forever, and should have this turned
off.  It is read when a session's provider is built.

@subsection In the brigade

A message sent to a @emph{running in-process} agent ---
@code{agent_send}, @code{cmacs-brigade-mailbox-send}, the dashboard ---
is steered into its tool loop instead of waiting for the turn to end.
The mailbox's rule is unchanged, with the steer's answer standing in
for ``the turn has started'':

@itemize @bullet
@item
the message is @emph{peeked}, sent, and left in the mailbox;
@item
@code{delivered} or @code{indeterminate} removes it (by identity, not
by position: @code{agent_drop} may have reordered the queue meanwhile)
and runs @code{cmacs-brigade-mailbox-delivered-functions};
@item
@code{refused} leaves it at the head, to become the next turn exactly
as before.
@end itemize

Only the head of the mailbox is offered, and the next message only once
the first has an answer: order is the mailbox's whole contract.  And a
turn that @emph{ends} while a steer is unanswered does not start the
next turn with that same message --- the steer's answer decides, which
is what stops the words running twice.

@vindex cmacs-brigade-steer-running
@code{cmacs-brigade-steer-running} (default @code{t}) turns this off
and restores wait-for-the-next-turn.  The subprocess workers are not
steered: the brigade spawned them with stdin already closed, so there
is no seam.

@subsection For programs

@findex cmacs-ai-harness-steer
@findex cmacs-ai-tools-steer
@multitable @columnfractions .40 .27 .33
@headitem Function @tab Steers @tab Answer arrives as
@item @code{(cmacs-ai-harness-steer @var{h} @var{text})}
@tab harness @var{h}'s running turn
@tab @code{(:steer @var{result} @var{reason} @var{ticket})} on its
event callback
@item @code{(cmacs-ai-tools-steer @var{e} @var{text} @var{fn})}
@tab the tool loop running on executor @var{e}
@tab the same list, as @var{fn}'s one argument
@end multitable

Both return a ticket number at once.  @code{cmacs-ai-harness-steer}
returns @code{nil} instead when the session has nothing that can be
steered; nothing was sent, and the caller queues.

@subsection Threads, and a lifetime bug this found

@code{ai_steerable_steer_async} may be called from any thread and
answers in the caller's thread-default main context.  Every caller in
cmacs is a DEFUN, so that is Emacs's thread --- but the tool loop being
steered runs on a worker with a private context, and ai-glib hops onto
it to splice the words in and hops back.  The C callback therefore does
no Lisp at all: it copies the answer into C memory and re-posts to the
cmacs context through @code{cmacs_glib_invoke_on_main}, the one place
cmacs guarantees both the Lisp thread and the @code{waiting_for_input}
guard.

The async user data is a ticket number, never a struct: the harness or
executor being steered can be freed while the answer is in flight, and
a ticket that fails to look up is the benign outcome.

Applying that rule turned up an existing bug.
@code{cmacs-ai-harness-send-input} passed the harness @emph{struct} as
the user data of @code{ai_conversation_send_input_async}.  Freeing a
busy harness cancels the conversation and frees that struct at once,
while the task --- which holds its own reference on the conversation
--- completes later and called back into freed memory.  Killing a
harness buffer mid-turn was enough to reach it.  It now passes the
integer handle and looks it up.

@node AI 0.13 Deferred
@section Deferred

ai-glib 0.13 also ships two things cmacs does not use yet.

@itemize @bullet
@item
@strong{Room mode and voiceprints.}  The library can follow a
multi-speaker room and tell speakers apart with
@code{AiOnnxSpeakerEmbedder}.  cmacs already has a speech engine with
speaker embedding and diarization --- @code{cmacs-sherpa}, on the
system onnxruntime --- so naming speakers here would be sherpa-backed
rather than a second embedder loaded beside it.  That is its own piece
of work and is not started.
@item
@strong{Everything under @file{deps/ai-glib/bin/}.}  Those are
ai-glib's own frontends (@command{ai}, @command{ai-tui} and friends).
cmacs consumes the library, not its programs, and ports nothing from
there.
@end itemize

Also not done, and worth knowing:

@itemize @bullet
@item
@strong{libreclaw's steer vfunc.}  libreclaw 0.30 added
@code{LcChannelInterface.send_steered} and
@code{LC_CHANNEL_CAPABILITY_STEER}, so a channel's far end can ask for
a message to steer a running turn and be told what became of it.  The
cmacs channel's @code{LcChannel} implementation lives in libreclaw
(@file{lc-cmacs-channel.c}), not in cmacs; teaching it to steer means a
change there (the vfunc, the capability, an inject that carries the
steer flag and a message id) before cmacs has anything to call.
@item
@strong{Auto-resume after a limit.}  A parked brigade run waits for
someone to say ``carry on''.  Doing that automatically is a policy
decision about spending a fresh allowance, and is left to you.
@end itemize

